let's get more reaction now to last  night's events joining me from new  jersey is writer for slate magazine  eamon ismail who was at the capitol  building hello to you eamon whereabouts  were you  and and just describe for us what  unfolded around you  i well first of all thank you so much  for having me  i am still in shock i had gotten home  last night very very late so i'm sorry  if i'm going to sound a little drowsy  but  i had gone to the ellipse park at 9 00  a.m yesterday morning  to document the um  you know stop the steel parade march  whatever you want to call it  and i was there when trump asked his  supporters to go to the capitol building  and uh take their country back right so  i followed them  for the 20 blocks that it would take to  get there and  it for the most part was weirdly  calm it felt like i was at comic con  people dressed up as their favorite  characters that they had seen on tv but  obviously as soon as we got to the  capitol building that's when things  started looking very very grim that's  when i started seeing  trump supporters attacking journalists  bullying them  i saw them smashing windows breaking  into the capitol building  shoving police officers you know uh  desecrating desecrating one of the most  important buildings in the country  and quite frankly uh as a reporter you  sort of just need to  stay in the moment and be aware so you  don't get hurt and make sure you're  doing your job correctly but  in the back of my mind i was in shock i  couldn't believe what i was seeing  and what about inside the building  eamonn  the inside the building was a mess there  were hundreds of protesters  going in and out there were there was  some police presence but for the most  part they were docile they didn't  respond to any of the violence they  didn't respond to  anybody breaking anything they tried to  shut the doors  and i've reported on tons and tons and  tons of  protests all over the country and even  outside the country and i've never seen  police  so restrained and if you ask me that's  what the story is here today that the  police  did not intervene they were unprepared  someone has to be held accountable but  you know people were going in going out  taking things rummaging through things  breaking things at their own will  and the police for the most part stood  by and watched and and this protest  didn't come as a surprise it had been  long talked about it was much planned  all week we have been hearing about  protesters planning to descend  on the capital so how were the police so  unprepared as you saw it that's right  uh one would think that they had the  most sophisticated counter-terrorism  measures  in play uh people use um  you know isis tweets and what they say  on  social media networks is intel but here  we have  trump supporters making their plans to  take have a hostile takeover the capitol  building public they were very  very transparent about what they were  planning they were calling it the second  american revolution yet the police were  so ill prepared  they weren't even dressed in riot gear  when the thousands of trump supporters  first ascended on the capitol building  campus it's astonishing it it really  it blows my mind uh i've covered black  lives matter protests  here uh this summer and police were  already in riot gear  in some cases outnumbering the  protesters this was  night and day and i could only have my  own suspicions as to why that was the  case  uh but for the sake of trying to stay  objective i'll keep it to myself  from the way you describe it that there  were there were two very distinct halves  you've written about inside the the  building  those more interested in taking selfies  with various props that they found  inside  versus the very much more aggressive  rioters  that's right the i would say the makeup  of the protesters  were honestly mostly cheerful  like they were happy to be there they  were celebrating people were playing  music on their allowed speakers uh  i'm pretty sure i was hearing the  immigrant song by led zeppelin  which uh is a little ironic but it's a  good song you know  people were there to celebrate people  brought their children there were  children inside that building  it was you know one of the interactions  that i think that will stick with me is  i talked to this  teenager he must have been 17 18 years  old from florida  and he described himself and the  protesters  there as peaceful and i said look in  front of your eyes what do you see  they're breaking the glass they're  kicking in the windows and jumping into  the capitol building  that's violence right he's like yeah i  guess so but it's not as bad as what  antifa does  you know i don't think that they fully  understand  what they were doing and to some degree  you have to  lay blame with the police officers they  were letting them in  they were letting them in i mean uh i  could see that there was some effort to  maintain  and keep people out maintain a perimeter  but they were so overwhelmed that there  was just nothing that they could do  except let them in  so you know uh it's not surprising to me  that some people who were  inside were taking their time going in  looking around  taking pictures leaving coming back  inside taking more pictures  celebrating leaving going back inside it  was  for several hours a lawless place yeah  and and that is the key isn't it the  amount of time  that it took to to regain some kind of  control ayman ismail thank you very much  indeed for getting up so early and  talking to us here on sky news
 

